Build PuTTY's AES-NI constants with _mm_setr_epi8 under clang and GCC.

WinSCP replaced _mm_setr_epi8 in aes-ni.c with a macro expanding to a brace
list, working around an MSVC link problem (its comment explains). That relies
on MSVC's __m128i, a union whose first member is 16 bytes: sixteen values fill
it byte by byte. Under clang and GCC __m128i is a vector of two 64-bit lanes,
so a brace list fills it lane by lane -- only the first two values are used,
each as a whole lane, and the excess is silently dropped. The counter
increment (1, 0, ...) happens to come out right; the byte-reversal shuffle
mask (15, 14, ...) does not, so AES-NI SDCTR produces wrong ciphertext, and an
SSH session using it fails with "Incoming packet was garbled on decryption"
(GCM: "Incorrect MAC"). Found by a first real session and confirmed against
the NIST SP 800-38A vector, which PuTTY's software AES passes.

MSVC keeps WinSCP's macro; clang and GCC, which have no such link problem,
use the intrinsic it stands in for. Three comment lines are joined to make
room for the conditional, so line counts are unchanged.

--- a/source/putty/crypto/aes-ni.c	2026-10-11 16:04:55.715647838 -0300
+++ b/source/putty/crypto/aes-ni.c	2026-10-11 16:04:55.735287405 -0300
@@ -139,17 +139,17 @@
     }
 }
 
-// WINSCP
-// WORKAROUND
+// WINSCP WORKAROUND
 // Cannot use _mm_setr_epi* - it results in the constant being stored in .rdata segment.
-// objconv reports:
-// Warning 1060: Different alignments specified for same segment, %s. Using highest alignment.rdata
-// Despite that the code crashes.
-// This macro is based on:
-// Based on https://stackoverflow.com/q/35268036/850848
+// objconv reports: Warning 1060: Different alignments specified for same segment, %s. Using highest alignment.rdata
+// Despite that the code crashes. This macro is based on: https://stackoverflow.com/q/35268036/850848
+#if !defined(__clang__) && !defined(__GNUC__) // linscp: MSVC's __m128i is a union filled byte by byte
 #define _MM_SETR_EPI8(a0, a1, a2, a3, a4, a5, a6, a7, a8, a9, aa, ab, ac, ad, ae, af) \
     { (char)a0, (char)a1, (char)a2, (char)a3, (char)a4, (char)a5, (char)a6, (char)a7, \
       (char)a8, (char)a9, (char)aa, (char)ab, (char)ac, (char)ad, (char)ae, (char)af }
+#else // linscp: clang's and GCC's is a vector of two 64-bit lanes, which a brace list fills lane by lane
+#define _MM_SETR_EPI8(a0, a1, a2, a3, a4, a5, a6, a7, a8, a9, aa, ab, ac, ad, ae, af) _mm_setr_epi8(a0, a1, a2, a3, a4, a5, a6, a7, a8, a9, aa, ab, ac, ad, ae, af)
+#endif
 
 /*
  * Auxiliary routine to increment the 128-bit counter used in SDCTR
